From fefa1c0e47baf301a6d6162d8c98256d8dd4146a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 13:35:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add six icon shapes for /tag: skull, X, sword, diamond,
 flame, star

Tags can show a colored arrow, the stop sign or the paw today. Raid
marking wants a few more distinct symbols, the familiar skull / X /
sword / diamond / flame / star set, so a caller can say "kill skull,
sheep diamond" and everyone sees a different shape.

- New shape keys 1 to 6 after '^' (skull, red X, gold sword, blue
  diamond, green flame, purple star), each with its own fixed color like
  the paw and stop sign. Older clients ignore an unknown key, so they
  still show the tag text, just without the shape.
- tag_shapes.cpp/.h (new): the geometry, with no DirectX dependency.
  Every shape is built from flat parts extruded to the same thickness as
  the octagon and paw. Each part is convex (or star-shaped about its
  center) so it triangulates as a simple fan; concave outlines come from
  overlapping parts, and detail (eye sockets, nose, teeth, the sword's
  fuller, the diamond's facet, the flame's core, the star's inner star)
  is a part standing slightly proud of both faces in a dark or light
  accent tone. No textures and no concave triangulation.
- TagArrows builds the meshes at startup, colors them with the existing
  face gradients plus the two accent tones, and sizes the vertex buffer
  to hold every shape at once alongside the arrow color cache.
- The shape is now picked from the tag's own color rather than after a
  nameplate color is substituted, so a nameplate color can no longer
  collide with a shape's color.
- Prettyprint, the target tooltip and /tag help name the new shapes.
- README: the new keys and an example.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
